WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.250
This is the executive briefing
for Book 10 of the Federal

3
00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.812
Modernization Workbook, Cloud
Boundary and Landing Zone.

4
00:00:07.812 --> 00:00:12.417
It's for the people who sign for
a cloud system or answer for it:

5
00:00:12.417 --> 00:00:15.824
authorizing officials,
ISSOs, CIOs and CISOs.

6
00:00:15.824 --> 00:00:18.175
The book's argument fits in one line.

7
00:00:18.175 --> 00:00:22.175
The license says what you bought;
the boundary says what you may use.

8
00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:26.962
A design that confuses the two fails in
front of the assessor, not in the lab.

9
00:00:26.962 --> 00:00:29.799
Over the next thirty minutes
I'll take four questions.

10
00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:33.142
Why the authorization boundary
decides what you can use,

11
00:00:33.142 --> 00:00:35.275
and why so much of it stays invisible.

12
00:00:35.275 --> 00:00:40.400
What changed at FedRAMP in 2026, and what
Government Community Cloud leaves out.

13
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.838
Who owns the boundary.

14
00:00:41.838 --> 00:00:45.011
And how to respond when a
capability falls outside it,

15
00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:47.824
and evidence that response
in your own package.

16
00:00:47.824 --> 00:00:50.312
There's no configuration on these slides.

17
00:00:50.312 --> 00:00:54.987
Each decision slide says what the decision
is, who owns it, and when you need it.

18
00:00:54.987 --> 00:00:58.400
Let's start with two
agencies and one license.

19
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:03.925
Picture two agencies with the
same Microsoft 365 license.

20
00:01:03.925 --> 00:01:07.890
One runs a commercial tenant, and
its admin center shows per-location

21
00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.925
network assessments, informed network
routing and an Adoption Score.

22
00:01:11.925 --> 00:01:15.185
The other runs a GCC tenant
and sees none of them,

23
00:01:15.185 --> 00:01:19.113
because Microsoft documents
each as unavailable there.

24
00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:23.212
Nothing in the license or the price
list tells the second agency that.

25
00:01:23.212 --> 00:01:26.704
The book's explanation is that
three separate decisions,

26
00:01:26.704 --> 00:01:30.938
by three different parties, have to
line up before a feature is usable.

27
00:01:30.938 --> 00:01:35.700
FedRAMP certifies a specific offering,
and the Marketplace is the record.

28
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.427
Microsoft decides what each
government environment includes,

29
00:01:39.427 --> 00:01:43.575
and publishes the differences one
feature at a time, with no single list.

30
00:01:43.575 --> 00:01:48.538
And the agency's authorizing official
accepts the risk of the agency's own use.

31
00:01:48.538 --> 00:01:51.826
What you can actually use is
the overlap of all three.

32
00:01:51.826 --> 00:01:54.525
So the first decision is simple and cheap.

33
00:01:54.525 --> 00:01:58.533
Before the next architecture
review or authorization milestone,

34
00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:03.488
require every cloud feature a design
depends on to be listed with its Marketplace

35
00:02:03.488 --> 00:02:07.787
package, its availability in your
environment, and the page that says so.

36
00:02:07.787 --> 00:02:12.676
Workload owners declare the dependencies;
the system owner checks each one.

37
00:02:12.676 --> 00:02:16.787
A feature with no citation is
unverified, not available.

38
00:02:16.787 --> 00:02:19.540
Next, why nobody sees this coming.

39
00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.990
Why does nobody see this coming?

40
00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.990
The book gives four reasons.

41
00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.912
Commercial and government
tenants share the same

42
00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:29.765
admin centers and much of
the same documentation.

43
00:02:29.765 --> 00:02:34.677
Exclusions are scattered, usually one
sentence on each feature's own page.

44
00:02:34.677 --> 00:02:39.016
Features move, so a gap list
from 2025 can be wrong today.

45
00:02:39.016 --> 00:02:41.215
And absence doesn't announce itself.

46
00:02:41.215 --> 00:02:45.190
A detection whose input never
arrives looks just like a quiet day.

47
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:49.557
Build a Zero Trust control on a signal
your environment doesn't deliver,

48
00:02:49.557 --> 00:02:51.703
and you haven't built a weak control.

49
00:02:51.703 --> 00:02:54.677
You've built no control,
and nothing tells you so.

50
00:02:54.677 --> 00:02:57.066
But the opposite error is just as costly.

51
00:02:57.066 --> 00:03:02.461
A widely repeated example listed five
features as silently missing from GCC.

52
00:03:02.461 --> 00:03:06.340
Checked against Microsoft's
documentation, only one is.

53
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.228
The others are gaps in
higher environments,

54
00:03:09.228 --> 00:03:12.678
aren't documented gaps, or
belong to Azure's own scope.

55
00:03:12.678 --> 00:03:18.490
An unverified boundary story sends an agency
to build compensations it doesn't need.

56
00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:23.565
The program's own register of
GCC telemetry gaps has 26 rows.

57
00:03:23.565 --> 00:03:27.052
Eight are vendor-confirmed;
fifteen are inference.

58
00:03:27.052 --> 00:03:32.277
So rebuild any gap list you rely on
so every row cites a vendor page.

59
00:03:32.277 --> 00:03:36.564
The system owner keeps it, and the
CISO decides what counts as evidence.

60
00:03:36.564 --> 00:03:39.090
Next, what changed at FedRAMP.

61
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:46.290
FedRAMP announced its Consolidated
Rules for 2026 on June 25, 2026.

62
00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:50.928
A cloud service offering is now FedRAMP
Certified, at a class from A to D.

63
00:03:50.928 --> 00:03:55.965
Class C includes the current Moderate
baseline, and Class D the current High.

64
00:03:55.965 --> 00:03:58.290
Two cautions come with the new labels.

65
00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:02.391
The class describes what FedRAMP
assessed, not your use; agencies may

66
00:04:02.391 --> 00:04:06.852
authorize a service at whatever security
category they deem appropriate.

67
00:04:06.852 --> 00:04:09.652
And certification doesn't
replace your decision.

68
00:04:09.652 --> 00:04:14.678
Each agency still authorizes its own
systems, and notifies FedRAMP afterward.

69
00:04:14.678 --> 00:04:16.328
The dates matter for planning.

70
00:04:16.328 --> 00:04:21.440
Adoption is mandatory for
everyone on January 1, 2027.

71
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:27.180
FedRAMP stops accepting new Rev5
certification requests on June 11, 2027,

72
00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:32.356
and says the end date for existing
Rev5 certifications isn't final,

73
00:04:32.356 --> 00:04:34.803
but is likely no later than 2029.

74
00:04:34.803 --> 00:04:39.403
Plan against those words, not against
a specific date someone repeats.

75
00:04:39.403 --> 00:04:42.627
So for years you'll hold two
kinds of package at once.

76
00:04:42.627 --> 00:04:46.127
Rev5 packages describe a
boundary the provider drew.

77
00:04:46.127 --> 00:04:49.715
Packages under the new rules
describe scope, as data.

78
00:04:49.715 --> 00:04:54.515
Read each in its own terms, and don't
force one vocabulary into the other.

79
00:04:54.515 --> 00:04:57.490
Next, what the word boundary now means.

80
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.190
The rules also changed what
the word boundary means.

81
00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:03.814
In the current machine-readable ruleset,

82
00:05:03.814 --> 00:05:07.365
the phrase authorization
boundary appears zero times.

83
00:05:07.365 --> 00:05:11.875
A provider's boundary is now four
sets of obligations: what's in scope,

84
00:05:11.875 --> 00:05:16.465
cryptography, vulnerability response,
and how evidence reaches you.

85
00:05:16.465 --> 00:05:20.240
The only boundary the rules name
is the agency's own system.

86
00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.865
The scope test is about
data, not network diagrams.

87
00:05:23.865 --> 00:05:27.152
A resource is in the offering
if it's likely to handle

88
00:05:27.152 --> 00:05:31.128
federal customer data or affect
its security, wherever it sits.

89
00:05:31.128 --> 00:05:34.588
So a refusal that rests on
raising the whole environment's

90
00:05:34.588 --> 00:05:37.615
level now has to show which
resource and which data.

91
00:05:37.615 --> 00:05:39.852
One line matters to every estate.

92
00:05:39.852 --> 00:05:43.645
Software installed on agency
systems, typically agents,

93
00:05:43.645 --> 00:05:48.566
application clients and mobile apps,
is entirely outside FedRAMP's scope.

94
00:05:48.566 --> 00:05:50.327
That doesn't make it unassessed.

95
00:05:50.327 --> 00:05:51.516
It makes it yours.

96
00:05:51.516 --> 00:05:56.285
So decide who owns each agent and
client, and cover it in the agency's

97
00:05:56.285 --> 00:06:01.402
own authorization: its configuration,
its updates and the data it sends.

98
00:06:01.402 --> 00:06:06.327
The authorizing official draws the boundary;
endpoint engineering does the work.

99
00:06:06.327 --> 00:06:09.081
Settle it at the next package update,

100
00:06:09.081 --> 00:06:13.303
and before the rules become
mandatory on January 1, 2027.

101
00:06:13.303 --> 00:06:15.720
Next, how changes are handled.

102
00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.986
Under the old model, a
significant change to

103
00:06:18.986 --> 00:06:21.782
a certified service went
back through approval.

104
00:06:21.782 --> 00:06:25.594
The new rules replace that with
notification, scaled to risk.

105
00:06:25.594 --> 00:06:29.157
Routine changes like patching
need no formal notice at all.

106
00:06:29.157 --> 00:06:32.804
Bigger changes are notified
within set business-day windows,

107
00:06:32.804 --> 00:06:37.308
and the most transformative ones need
plans filed weeks before work starts.

108
00:06:37.308 --> 00:06:40.919
A change of certification class
still needs a new assessment.

109
00:06:40.919 --> 00:06:42.795
Evidence becomes a feed too.

110
00:06:42.795 --> 00:06:46.258
Providers must run a
FedRAMP-compatible trust center

111
00:06:46.258 --> 00:06:49.645
with programmatic access
to all certification data.

112
00:06:49.645 --> 00:06:57.883
On October 21, 2026, only 110 of 781
Marketplace listings had a trust-center entry.

113
00:06:57.883 --> 00:06:59.782
There are two consequences for you.

114
00:06:59.782 --> 00:07:04.338
A provider that ships continuously can
treat most releases as routine and file

115
00:07:04.338 --> 00:07:09.032
nothing, so keeping current with commercial
no longer costs it a compliance cycle.

116
00:07:09.032 --> 00:07:11.369
But your review duty doesn't shrink.

117
00:07:11.369 --> 00:07:14.484
Agencies must review each
ongoing certification

118
00:07:14.484 --> 00:07:17.820
report for changes that affect
the risk they accepted.

119
00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.370
So name the reviewer now.

120
00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:23.720
FedRAMP recommends a federal senior
information security official;

121
00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.245
in the program's model that's
the CISO or a named delegate.

122
00:07:27.245 --> 00:07:30.914
The ISSO reads each notice
against the boundary record,

123
00:07:30.914 --> 00:07:35.858
and the authorizing official decides
whether it changes the agency's risk.

124
00:07:35.858 --> 00:07:38.420
Next, the feature gap itself.

125
00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.185
Microsoft's own documents
don't describe GCC

126
00:07:42.185 --> 00:07:45.282
as a dimmed version of
the commercial service.

127
00:07:45.282 --> 00:07:50.382
GCC is the worldwide service, minus
a published list of exclusions.

128
00:07:50.382 --> 00:07:55.119
The restricted, separately networked
environment begins at GCC High.

129
00:07:55.119 --> 00:07:56.920
And that list isn't a ladder.

130
00:07:56.920 --> 00:08:01.572
Several features are missing in
GCC, GCC High and DoD alike,

131
00:08:01.572 --> 00:08:04.770
so moving up doesn't restore everything.

132
00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:08.770
The largest documented loss is
the network connectivity layer.

133
00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.949
A commercial tenant gets,
at no extra license cost,

134
00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:16.755
a daily network score for each location,
a view of its egress and front doors,

135
00:08:16.755 --> 00:08:19.701
insights such as detecting
TLS interception,

136
00:08:19.701 --> 00:08:23.345
and performance attributed
to named network providers.

137
00:08:23.345 --> 00:08:27.593
An agency choosing between SASE or
transport providers in a commercial

138
00:08:27.593 --> 00:08:32.120
tenant gets that comparative evidence
from Microsoft, in its own admin center.

139
00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:35.395
The same agency in GCC gets none of it.

140
00:08:35.395 --> 00:08:38.773
Microsoft's connectivity
test tool does run in GCC,

141
00:08:38.773 --> 00:08:42.795
and it closes part of the gap,
but not the provider comparison.

142
00:08:42.795 --> 00:08:45.045
So this decision is a budget line.

143
00:08:45.045 --> 00:08:48.475
Fund the independent measurement
that replaces that layer,

144
00:08:48.475 --> 00:08:51.082
before you pick a
transport or SASE provider.

145
00:08:51.082 --> 00:08:55.096
That's the sponsor and CIO's call,
because they fund the platform.

146
00:08:55.096 --> 00:08:58.430
Next, why a higher
certification won't fix it.

147
00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:02.615
Much federal reasoning runs
like this: GCC is Moderate,

148
00:09:02.615 --> 00:09:07.805
Moderate is why the feature is missing, so
a High certification would bring it back.

149
00:09:07.805 --> 00:09:10.305
The Marketplace doesn't
support that chain.

150
00:09:10.305 --> 00:09:17.617
Read on October 21, 2026, it lists
three Microsoft 365 government packages.

151
00:09:17.617 --> 00:09:21.604
The GCC package agencies
run is certified at Class C,

152
00:09:21.604 --> 00:09:24.293
Moderate, with 100 authorizations.

153
00:09:24.293 --> 00:09:29.743
A second package with the identical name
is In Process at Class D, High, with none.

154
00:09:29.743 --> 00:09:34.543
And GCC High is a separate package,
already certified at Class D.

155
00:09:34.543 --> 00:09:37.943
The Marketplace doesn't say
how those first two relate,

156
00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:41.343
so treat it as a question for
the provider, not a roadmap.

157
00:09:41.343 --> 00:09:42.642
Here's the key point.

158
00:09:42.642 --> 00:09:49.368
Network connectivity is missing in GCC
High too, and GCC High is already Class D.

159
00:09:49.368 --> 00:09:52.579
A higher class changes what data
you may put in the service.

160
00:09:52.579 --> 00:09:56.692
It doesn't, by itself, restore
a feature the provider excluded.

161
00:09:56.692 --> 00:10:00.187
An agency that defers an equivalent
because High is coming may

162
00:10:00.187 --> 00:10:03.817
wait for something the certification
was never going to deliver.

163
00:10:03.817 --> 00:10:08.132
So plan against the class of the package
you actually use, and don't hard-code

164
00:10:08.132 --> 00:10:12.168
Moderate into a multi-year architecture
either, because that may move.

165
00:10:12.168 --> 00:10:14.346
The sponsor and CIO own that plan,

166
00:10:14.346 --> 00:10:17.817
in the current architecture
and acquisition cycle.

167
00:10:17.817 --> 00:10:20.210
Next, who owns the boundary.

168
00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.548
The book's rule is one decider per row,
and it starts from FedRAMP's split.

169
00:10:25.548 --> 00:10:29.061
The provider owns the service
boundary and its certification.

170
00:10:29.061 --> 00:10:31.942
The agency owns the
boundary of its own system,

171
00:10:31.942 --> 00:10:35.735
and everything it builds,
configures and connects inside it.

172
00:10:35.735 --> 00:10:40.213
On the agency side, the authorizing
official determines the boundary,

173
00:10:40.213 --> 00:10:42.297
with input from the system owner.

174
00:10:42.297 --> 00:10:44.661
The official signs every exception and

175
00:10:44.661 --> 00:10:47.597
decides whether a change
needs reauthorization.

176
00:10:47.597 --> 00:10:49.957
That risk acceptance can't be delegated,

177
00:10:49.957 --> 00:10:52.673
and the role is for
government personnel only.

178
00:10:52.673 --> 00:10:56.360
The system owner keeps the
boundary record and the package.

179
00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:01.364
The CISO sets the control requirements
and decides what counts as evidence,

180
00:11:01.364 --> 00:11:03.710
and the ISSO reads provider notices.

181
00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:08.421
The sponsor and CIO choose the clouds,
fund the platform and break the ties,

182
00:11:08.421 --> 00:11:13.210
which will happen, because everyone
can reasonably claim the landing zone.

183
00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:14.898
Then the people who do the work.

184
00:11:14.898 --> 00:11:17.564
The platform team builds the landing zone,

185
00:11:17.564 --> 00:11:20.459
but doesn't decide whether
a gap is acceptable.

186
00:11:20.459 --> 00:11:24.047
Workload owners declare the
features their designs depend on.

187
00:11:24.047 --> 00:11:27.698
And only a contracting officer
can change what the agency bought.

188
00:11:27.698 --> 00:11:30.372
FedRAMP is consulted, not in charge.

189
00:11:30.372 --> 00:11:34.723
Its certification is an input to
the authorizing official's decision,

190
00:11:34.723 --> 00:11:36.373
never a replacement for it.

191
00:11:36.373 --> 00:11:39.400
Next, decisions that hide as tickets.

192
00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:44.588
The most consequential boundary decisions
don't look like boundary decisions.

193
00:11:44.588 --> 00:11:46.343
They look like engineering tickets,

194
00:11:46.343 --> 00:11:49.212
and that's how they slip past
the people who should make them.

195
00:11:49.212 --> 00:11:50.275
Take placement.

196
00:11:50.275 --> 00:11:53.152
Vending a new subscription
is a platform task,

197
00:11:53.152 --> 00:11:58.225
but whether a workload is inside the
authorized system is the system owner's call.

198
00:11:58.225 --> 00:12:01.435
The cheapest way to turn
an authorized system into

199
00:12:01.435 --> 00:12:04.875
an unauthorized one is a
subscription no one recorded.

200
00:12:04.875 --> 00:12:09.475
So a workload whose subscription isn't
in the boundary record doesn't go live.

201
00:12:09.475 --> 00:12:10.725
Then the contract.

202
00:12:10.725 --> 00:12:16.301
GCC coverage requires a GCC SKU, so a
licensing choice is a boundary choice,

203
00:12:16.301 --> 00:12:19.500
and it belongs to the contracting officer.

204
00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:23.201
Provider changes that might affect
the agency's risk go to the

205
00:12:23.201 --> 00:12:26.975
authorizing official, not whoever
is on the distribution list.

206
00:12:26.975 --> 00:12:29.618
And agencies can't
demand more than FedRAMP

207
00:12:29.618 --> 00:12:32.188
requires without a formal finding of need.

208
00:12:32.188 --> 00:12:36.262
The landing zone makes these decisions
checkable, but it doesn't make them.

209
00:12:36.262 --> 00:12:38.188
Two planning facts for leaders.

210
00:12:38.188 --> 00:12:42.600
Microsoft's landing-zone accelerators
are supported only in public Azure,

211
00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:46.087
so an Azure Government build
needs the code adapted by hand.

212
00:12:46.087 --> 00:12:49.896
And the pipeline that builds it is
part of the boundary question too,

213
00:12:49.896 --> 00:12:51.937
because its state data is sensitive.

214
00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:54.101
Plan both; don't discover them.

215
00:12:54.101 --> 00:12:57.610
Next, what to do when a
capability is outside.

216
00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.062
When a capability is outside the boundary,

217
00:13:01.062 --> 00:13:05.385
the book gives three honest responses,
each a decision with an owner.

218
00:13:05.385 --> 00:13:08.110
One: compensate inside the boundary.

219
00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.682
Rebuild the capability, or enough of it,

220
00:13:10.682 --> 00:13:14.460
from signals you do have, and
say plainly that it's derived.

221
00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:17.602
For the network gap,
scheduled runs of Microsoft's

222
00:13:17.602 --> 00:13:20.285
test tool close part of
it, labeled derived.

223
00:13:20.285 --> 00:13:25.460
A narrative that hides the substitution is
exactly the kind an assessor takes apart.

224
00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:27.735
Two: a documented exception.

225
00:13:27.735 --> 00:13:30.513
Either a request through
the provider's process,

226
00:13:30.513 --> 00:13:34.472
or the authorizing official accepting
the residual risk in writing.

227
00:13:34.472 --> 00:13:39.311
A gap you intend to close goes on the plan
of action and milestones, with an owner.

228
00:13:39.311 --> 00:13:43.653
A gap you'll live with gets a signed
exception with an end or review date,

229
00:13:43.653 --> 00:13:47.472
so that permanent is a decision
someone repeats, not a default.

230
00:13:47.472 --> 00:13:51.448
Three: an alternative path, a
different certified service.

231
00:13:51.448 --> 00:13:55.673
And going without is legitimate
too, as long as someone decided it.

232
00:13:55.673 --> 00:13:57.622
Before any of these, probe.

233
00:13:57.622 --> 00:14:00.241
Test what actually works
in your own tenant,

234
00:14:00.241 --> 00:14:03.297
on a schedule and after
relevant provider notices.

235
00:14:03.297 --> 00:14:06.642
So for each gap, the system
owner proposes a response,

236
00:14:06.642 --> 00:14:09.987
the authorizing official
decides every exception,

237
00:14:09.987 --> 00:14:15.122
and the CISO sets the evidence class,
before a design depends on the capability.

238
00:14:15.122 --> 00:14:17.980
Next, two cautions on exceptions.

239
00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:20.518
Two cautions on exceptions.

240
00:14:20.518 --> 00:14:22.567
First, the provider's path.

241
00:14:22.567 --> 00:14:26.918
ExpressRoute for Microsoft
365 is the worked example.

242
00:14:26.918 --> 00:14:30.755
Microsoft doesn't recommend it,
and authorizes it only when a

243
00:14:30.755 --> 00:14:34.668
regulation mandates direct
connectivity, quoted, with a link.

244
00:14:34.668 --> 00:14:39.018
Federal readers often assume
FedRAMP or FISMA supplies that hook.

245
00:14:39.018 --> 00:14:42.438
The program hasn't found a
rule or provision that does,

246
00:14:42.438 --> 00:14:46.092
and TIC 3.0 mandates no
specific technology.

247
00:14:46.092 --> 00:14:49.729
Write it into a task order before
the authorization is in hand,

248
00:14:49.729 --> 00:14:51.868
and you may find the gate afterwards.

249
00:14:51.868 --> 00:14:55.252
But the process is a good
template for any request:

250
00:14:55.252 --> 00:14:57.893
a named requirement, quoted, with a link.

251
00:14:57.893 --> 00:15:03.092
Second, the scope arguments the 2026
rules allow each come with a limit.

252
00:15:03.092 --> 00:15:07.031
Agents are outside FedRAMP, but
not outside your authorization.

253
00:15:07.031 --> 00:15:11.968
Routine changes go unnotified, but you
still decide whether they affect your risk.

254
00:15:11.968 --> 00:15:16.181
And acquisition is where an exception
path gets closed by accident.

255
00:15:16.181 --> 00:15:20.553
Microsoft's Network Provider Program
is no longer open to new providers,

256
00:15:20.553 --> 00:15:25.655
so a solicitation that requires membership
restricts competition to a closed pool.

257
00:15:25.655 --> 00:15:30.005
Specify the capability, not the
product that happens to meet it today.

258
00:15:30.005 --> 00:15:33.375
The system owner decides whether
to make a provider request,

259
00:15:33.375 --> 00:15:36.880
and that decision belongs before
the solicitation is written.

260
00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:39.330
Next, the alternative path.

261
00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:43.692
The third response is a different
certified service for the workload.

262
00:15:43.692 --> 00:15:46.012
The book works through collaboration,

263
00:15:46.012 --> 00:15:50.143
and its first rule is to read the
certification, not the announcement.

264
00:15:50.143 --> 00:15:54.062
On the Marketplace, Google
Workspace is certified at Class D,

265
00:15:54.062 --> 00:15:58.215
while Gemini for Government is a
separate listing at Class B, Low,

266
00:15:58.215 --> 00:16:03.231
even though Google says Gemini in
Workspace apps is FedRAMP High authorized.

267
00:16:03.231 --> 00:16:07.484
Which features sit inside which
certification is a question for the package,

268
00:16:07.484 --> 00:16:10.142
and details like that decide
what you can turn on.

269
00:16:10.142 --> 00:16:12.493
The public record shows both outcomes.

270
00:16:12.493 --> 00:16:18.223
In September 2025 a cabinet-level department
signed a five-year agreement worth up

271
00:16:18.223 --> 00:16:23.456
to 89 million dollars to move more than
50,000 employees to Google Workspace,

272
00:16:23.456 --> 00:16:26.529
with a temporary 71
percent price reduction.

273
00:16:26.529 --> 00:16:29.668
So a five-year model has to
price the renewal years.

274
00:16:29.668 --> 00:16:34.041
A military department went the
other way, ending about 150,000

275
00:16:34.041 --> 00:16:38.756
Google email licenses after an
audit found about 16 percent in use.

276
00:16:38.756 --> 00:16:41.705
Measure adoption in the
pilot; don't assume it.

277
00:16:41.705 --> 00:16:45.505
The choice is only safe if
the identity plane stays put.

278
00:16:45.505 --> 00:16:49.539
Entra ID remains the source,
and the suite consumes identity,

279
00:16:49.539 --> 00:16:52.443
with Conditional Access
applied at sign-in.

280
00:16:52.443 --> 00:16:56.954
The sponsor and CIO make the suite
decision as its own approval, separate

281
00:16:56.954 --> 00:17:01.842
from identity modernization, and read
the listing again on the day they decide.

282
00:17:01.842 --> 00:17:04.350
Next, what to ask your provider.

283
00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:07.863
The book turns all of this into requests.

284
00:17:07.863 --> 00:17:10.387
Give us commercial parity
can't be answered.

285
00:17:10.387 --> 00:17:15.331
A specific question tied to a
published rule or exclusion can,

286
00:17:15.331 --> 00:17:17.076
with a yes, a no or a date.

287
00:17:17.076 --> 00:17:21.289
Three habits make a request
hard to deflect: name one item,

288
00:17:21.289 --> 00:17:25.413
cite the basis with a link, and
ask for data, not documents.

289
00:17:25.413 --> 00:17:28.426
There are eight requests,
and the themes are clear.

290
00:17:28.426 --> 00:17:33.613
Publish the exclusion list as data, and
name the exception path for each item.

291
00:17:33.613 --> 00:17:38.974
Restore the network observability
layer in GCC, or publish the obstacle.

292
00:17:38.974 --> 00:17:42.388
Explain the two same-named GCC packages.

293
00:17:42.388 --> 00:17:45.999
List the agents and clients
outside the certified offering.

294
00:17:45.999 --> 00:17:50.237
And deliver a trust center and change
notices in machine-readable form.

295
00:17:50.237 --> 00:17:54.224
On October 21, 2026, none
of the three Microsoft

296
00:17:54.224 --> 00:17:58.525
365 government packages
had a trust-center entry.

297
00:17:58.525 --> 00:18:02.667
Five of the eight rest on
obligations the 2026 rules already

298
00:18:02.667 --> 00:18:06.312
place on providers, so none
needs a boundary argument.

299
00:18:06.312 --> 00:18:09.957
And asking isn't requiring:
requiring more than FedRAMP

300
00:18:09.957 --> 00:18:13.838
does takes a determination by
the agency head or a delegate.

301
00:18:13.838 --> 00:18:18.528
The system owner makes these requests,
with acquisition, once the feature

302
00:18:18.528 --> 00:18:23.912
inventory exists, and records each request
and answer in the authorization package.

303
00:18:23.912 --> 00:18:26.387
A documented no is evidence too.

304
00:18:26.387 --> 00:18:28.680
Finally, your own package.

305
00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:32.418
An agency never authorizes
a cloud service;

306
00:18:32.418 --> 00:18:35.405
it authorizes its own
system, which uses one.

307
00:18:35.405 --> 00:18:38.530
The provider's certification
is evidence you read.

308
00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:40.669
Your package is evidence you write,

309
00:18:40.669 --> 00:18:44.430
and it should contain only what
the agency is responsible for.

310
00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:49.122
FedRAMP is blunt: don't copy and paste
provider controls into your plan,

311
00:18:49.122 --> 00:18:54.543
and don't blindly copy provider findings
into your plan of action and milestones.

312
00:18:54.543 --> 00:18:57.254
The program writes the boundary down once,

313
00:18:57.254 --> 00:19:01.205
as a record the system owner keeps,
and every artifact cites it.

314
00:19:01.205 --> 00:19:03.718
The system description names the boundary.

315
00:19:03.718 --> 00:19:08.055
Each external service is named
with its package, class and SKU.

316
00:19:08.055 --> 00:19:09.880
Each crossing gets an agreement.

317
00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:12.509
When something changes,
one record changes,

318
00:19:12.509 --> 00:19:15.430
and every narrative that
cites it stays current.

319
00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:17.080
Know whose package you're reading.

320
00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:21.585
The GCC listing says it leverages
Azure Government as its infrastructure,

321
00:19:21.585 --> 00:19:26.018
so inheritance runs through two provider
packages before it reaches you.

322
00:19:26.018 --> 00:19:30.462
A compensating narrative takes four
sentences: what the control needs,

323
00:19:30.462 --> 00:19:34.378
what the provider documents as
unavailable, what runs instead,

324
00:19:34.378 --> 00:19:37.843
marked derived, and where the
remaining gap is recorded.

325
00:19:37.843 --> 00:19:40.946
The system owner builds this
before the package reaches

326
00:19:40.946 --> 00:19:44.181
the authorizing official, and
keeps it current from then on.

327
00:19:44.181 --> 00:19:46.790
To close, the five decisions.

328
00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:50.440
So, five decisions, each with its owner.

329
00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:55.210
The authorizing official determines
the boundary before workloads arrive,

330
00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:58.415
and signs every exception
with an end or review date.

331
00:19:58.415 --> 00:20:02.936
The system owner keeps the boundary
record, gates every new subscription,

332
00:20:02.936 --> 00:20:06.715
and checks every feature dependency
before a design is built.

333
00:20:06.715 --> 00:20:10.586
The CISO names the reviewer of
provider change notices and

334
00:20:10.586 --> 00:20:14.690
decides what counts as evidence,
with the ISSO doing the reading.

335
00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:19.679
The sponsor and CIO fund the measurement
and compensations the register calls for,

336
00:20:19.679 --> 00:20:24.090
and plan against the class you actually
have, not the one you're waiting for.

337
00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:28.039
And the contracting officer buys
the SKU the boundary assumes,

338
00:20:28.039 --> 00:20:31.077
and specifies capabilities,
not program badges.

339
00:20:31.077 --> 00:20:32.990
The next step needs no award.

340
00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:38.134
Before the next architecture review, list
every cloud feature your designs depend

341
00:20:38.134 --> 00:20:39.327
on, with its package,

342
00:20:39.327 --> 00:20:43.203
its availability in your environment
and the page that says so.

343
00:20:43.203 --> 00:20:47.102
Then put the book's requests to your
provider, and record the answers.

344
00:20:47.102 --> 00:20:51.656
The rules become mandatory
on January 1, 2027,

345
00:20:51.656 --> 00:20:55.840
and new Rev5 requests
stop on June 11, 2027.

346
00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:58.302
Neither date moves your own boundary.

347
00:20:58.302 --> 00:21:01.889
It's still yours to draw,
to evidence and to sign.

348
00:21:01.889 --> 00:21:05.890
The license says what you bought;
the boundary says what you may use.

349
00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:09.740
Book 10 has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

350
00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:10.550
Thank you.
